 "notes": [
  {
   "id": "np11-01",
   "cat": "grammar",
   "label": "Imperative",
   "quote": "quin morere ut merita es",
   "trans": "no, die as you deserve",
   "line": "547",
   "body": "Dido gives herself an order, as if she were her own judge passing sentence. The abrupt imperative cuts off all the alternatives she has just considered.",
   "exam": ""
  },
  {
   "id": "np11-02",
   "cat": "imagery",
   "label": "Characterisation",
   "quote": "ut merita es",
   "trans": "as you deserve",
   "line": "547",
   "body": "Dido believes she has earned death. Her guilt over Sychaeus has turned into self-condemnation, so the reader sees how completely she now blames herself.",
   "exam": ""
  },
  {
   "id": "np11-03",
   "cat": "diction",
   "label": "Euphemism",
   "quote": "ferroque averte dolorem",
   "trans": "and turn away your grief with the sword",
   "line": "547",
   "body": "Suicide is described as a cure for pain, which shows that Dido now sees death as a release rather than a punishment.",
   "exam": ""
  },
  {
   "id": "np11-04",
   "cat": "structure",
   "label": "Anaphora",
   "quote": "tu lacrimis evicta meis, tu prima",
   "trans": "you, overcome by my tears, you first",
   "line": "548",
   "body": "The repeated tu turns Dido's anger on Anna. The accusation is unfair, since Dido herself wanted Anna's advice, which shows a mind looking for someone to blame.",
   "exam": ""
  },
  {
   "id": "np11-05",
   "cat": "order",
   "label": "Emphatic position",
   "quote": "furentem",
   "trans": "raging",
   "line": "548",
   "body": "The participle stands at the end of line 548, so Dido's madness is the last word before the burden of suffering is placed on her.",
   "exam": ""
  },
  {
   "id": "np11-06",
   "cat": "diction",
   "label": "Apostrophe",
   "quote": "germana",
   "trans": "sister",
   "line": "549",
   "body": "The affectionate word sits in the middle of an accusation, which shows Dido torn between love for Anna and resentment.",
   "exam": ""
  },
  {
   "id": "np11-07",
   "cat": "structure",
   "label": "Pairing",
   "quote": "oneras atque obicis hosti",
   "trans": "you load me down and throw me to the enemy",
   "line": "549",
   "body": "Two verbs of violence present Anna as an attacker. Calling Aeneas hosti, an enemy, shows how far Dido's feelings have turned.",
   "exam": ""
  },
  {
   "id": "np11-08",
   "cat": "structure",
   "label": "Anaphora",
   "quote": "non licuit … non servata fides",
   "trans": "I was not allowed … faith was not kept",
   "line": "550–552",
   "body": "Two sentences open with non, setting out what Dido has lost: an innocent single life, and her loyalty to her dead husband.",
   "exam": ""
  },
  {
   "id": "np11-09",
   "cat": "imagery",
   "label": "Comparison",
   "quote": "more ferae",
   "trans": "in the manner of a wild creature",
   "line": "551",
   "body": "Dido envies the freedom of a wild animal, which lives without marriage or guilt. The image recalls the wounded deer of lines 68–73 and suggests a life outside human ties.",
   "exam": ""
  },
  {
   "id": "np11-10",
   "cat": "order",
   "label": "Emphatic position",
   "quote": "Sychaeo",
   "trans": "to Sychaeus",
   "line": "552",
   "body": "Dido's speech ends on her dead husband's name, so the reader is left with the vow she has broken.",
   "exam": ""
  },
  {
   "id": "np11-11",
   "cat": "diction",
   "label": "Hyperbole",
   "quote": "tantos … questus",
   "trans": "such great laments",
   "line": "553",
   "body": "Virgil stresses the size of her grief, and rumpebat, burst out, makes it sound violent, as if her heart could not hold it.",
   "exam": ""
  },
  {
   "id": "np11-12",
   "cat": "structure",
   "label": "Contrast",
   "quote": "Aeneas celsa in puppi iam certus eundi carpebat somnos",
   "trans": "Aeneas, high on the stern, now set on leaving, was enjoying his sleep",
   "line": "554–555",
   "body": "The scene cuts straight from Dido's sleepless despair to Aeneas asleep and calm. The contrast makes his peace of mind seem cold beside her torment.",
   "exam": ""
  },
  {
   "id": "np11-13",
   "cat": "structure",
   "label": "Repetition",
   "quote": "iam … iam",
   "trans": "now … now",
   "line": "554–555",
   "body": "The repeated iam stresses that Aeneas is ready and his mind is made up, while Dido is still struggling.",
   "exam": ""
  },
  {
   "id": "np11-14",
   "cat": "diction",
   "label": "Characterisation",
   "quote": "rite paratis",
   "trans": "duly prepared",
   "line": "555",
   "body": "Rite suggests that everything has been done properly, even religiously. Aeneas is shown as orderly and dutiful, which sharpens the difference between him and the disordered Dido.",
   "exam": ""
  },
  {
   "id": "np11-15",
   "cat": "structure",
   "label": "Polysyndeton",
   "quote": "vocemque coloremque et crines flavos et membra decora",
   "trans": "his voice and colouring and golden hair and graceful limbs",
   "line": "558–559",
   "body": "The list joined by -que and et lingers on the god's appearance item by item, which gives the dream vision a vivid, solid presence.",
   "exam": ""
  },
  {
   "id": "np11-16",
   "cat": "sound",
   "label": "Metre",
   "quote": "vocemque coloremque",
   "trans": "his voice and his colouring",
   "line": "558",
   "body": "The double -que makes line 558 hypermetric: its final syllable elides into the next line. The line seems to spill over, much as the vision floods into Aeneas' sleep.",
   "exam": ""
  },
  {
   "id": "np11-17",
   "cat": "note",
   "label": "Sychaeus",
   "quote": "cineri promissa Sychaeo",
   "trans": "promised to the ashes of Sychaeus",
   "line": "552",
   "body": "Dido's husband at Tyre, murdered for his wealth by her brother Pygmalion. After his death she vowed never to remarry (4.24–29), and her shame at breaking that vow runs through Book 4.",
   "exam": ""
  },
  {
   "id": "np11-18",
   "cat": "note",
   "label": "Mercury",
   "quote": "Mercurio",
   "trans": "to Mercury",
   "line": "558",
   "body": "The messenger god, son of Jupiter. His first visit (4.265–276) ordered Aeneas to leave Carthage; this dream repeats the command with fresh urgency.",
   "exam": ""
  }
 ],
 "section": 11,
 "questions": [
  "Dido blames herself, Anna and her broken vow to Sychaeus in quick succession. Whose fault does she finally think her suffering is, and how far does Virgil invite us to agree?",
  "Compare the sleeping Aeneas in lines 554–555 with the sleepless Dido of the lines before. What effect does Virgil achieve by setting the two scenes side by side?",
  "Why do you think Mercury appears to Aeneas a second time, and in a dream?"
 ],
 "context": [
  {
   "h": "Dido's night",
   "body": "<p>This passage closes the long night that begins at line 522, where Virgil contrasts the calm of the whole sleeping world with Dido, who cannot rest. Her monologue (lines 534–552) moves through every option left to her: to go back to her old suitors, to follow the Trojans, to sail after them with her people. Each is rejected, and she ends where she began, with death.</p><p>Aeneas, by contrast, has already decided. He has been visited once by Mercury (lines 265–276), sent by Jupiter to remind him of Italy and of Ascanius' future. Now the god appears again, this time in a dream, to tell him that the moment to leave is now.</p>"
  }
 ]
}